Z||000003||Loki||11-12-2001||03:55 PM||||Ya know. I didnt even think about till I wrote it, my bad. Dont worry what I got was cheap mem anyway. I should have paid more   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Oh yeah, flag THANK YOU!!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  better?  HEheh<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/fieroloki/lokisig1%20copy.jpg">  <BR>"All Fieros broke right now" :(||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||11-12-2001||04:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>I ordered a set of 512MB PC133 SDRAM<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is that "set" as in 2-256MB sticks? Or 1-512MB? If it's a single 512MB, there could be your problem. There's nothin magic about Apple memory either.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MinnGreenGT||11-12-2001||07:18 PM||||A SET as in one pair of 512MB "sticks" totalling 1024MB or 1GB of RAM.  The 3 newest versions of Apple's G4 Systems use either PC100 or PC133 SDRAM (depending on model) utilizing max chip sets of 512MB per DIMM.  The system has 4 slots, but will only recognize a max total of 1.5GB of RAM.  And if you search Apple's Knoweledge Base, you'll find notices about the 3 most recent Firmware upgrades being picky about RAM "...any Non-Spec RAM will not be recognized by the system..."<P>Trust me.  I've done my homework.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||11-12-2001||07:24 PM||||<B>Sold!</B> (in the mall...)<P>Thanks guys!||||reg||1
Z||000007||kslish||11-12-2001||07:48 PM||||MinnGreenGT, I may have the solution to your problem!!<P>DIMM First Aid can detect the proper settings for and reflash the EEPROMs on non-Apple spec DIMMs (as long as they are the proper type) so they will work fine in your machine.  I've been through this same problem on my sister's iMac and ran DIMM First Aid and all the generic (i.e. cheap) memory I added is now correctly recognized.  <P>You can dowload DIMM First Aid from:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.mactcp.org.nz/dimmfirstaid.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mactcp.org.nz/dimmfirstaid.html</A> <P>and read some background info on the app at: <BR> <A HREF="http://www.versiontracker.com/moreinfo.fcgi?id=10415&db=mac" TARGET=_blank>http://www.versiontracker.com/moreinfo.fcgi?id=10415&db=mac</A> <P>It's a really tiny, quick and easy program to run, and it will run a test and tell you whether or not it can reflash the DIMM's EEPROM to get it to work and will ask you if you want to continue (it's pretty foolproof).  <P>Worth a shot and a lot easier than trying to find "Apple approved" DIMMs.<P>Ken S.<P>Oh yea, since I'm a part time (very part time) Apple employee (I do beta testing and a little bit of sales, but only when they decide they need me), my advice is not necessarily endorsed by Apple Computer and all that legal B.S.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||thomas_l||11-13-2001||09:05 AM||||OK I stand corrected - there is some magic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. That post about the EEPROM flash is good stuff to remember for later. I had never heard of this with Apples probably because I don't use them much. I have done tech support on them on rare occaisions and have a couple of creaky 6100 PowerMacs at home. With those, you can pop in just about any EDO - just no more than 64MB. The Intel servers I work with daily do require OEM spec RAM so I feel your pain. At least you can d/l a program to "cheat" the spec.||||reg||1
